Z||000000||Pheagey||12-11-2001||06:13 PM||||Ok, ok. Actually it IS melting. Err. bla...<P>The rubber covering the + batter terminla is metling anytime the engine gets hot. I have to cable running from the battery to the outter fire wall, then back to the engine. The rubber is just metling off! The last time this happend I had to leave my car and it got brokin into and I'm still recovering from that! Suggestions please...<BR>Thanks...<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||10
Z||000001||REDHOTT88||12-11-2001||06:22 PM||||WOW... have you tried Trojans?<P>Maybe you should slow down a little and try lubrication!!! Ha Ha  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Seriously, I've heard of the terminal wires melting when trying to start a cold, dead engine and when the cable is too small for the current. Other than that...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/confused.gif"> <P>------------------<BR>Steve<BR><B>1988 Formula 2.8L</B> V6 5 speed <BR><FONT COLOR="ffoooo"> (Red and shiny all over!)</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000002||avengador1||12-11-2001||07:02 PM||||The wire could be getting hot because it is not making a good connection. This causes a lot of resistance and makes the wire heat up. It could also be caused by a short in the battery causing the same situation when the battery is trying to charge up. Make sure your connections are clean and that the wire is the right gauge and in good condition.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Avendgador1.jpg">  <p>[This message has been edited by avengador1 (edited 12-11-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnt671||12-11-2001||07:19 PM||||I got a long cable and ran it along the throttle cable and then down behind the heat shield over the heater hoses and down to the starter.  Be very careful not to have it to near any sharp edges and use lots of wire ties.  This kept it away from the engine heat to not melt the wire.  If it is not heat related melting the do as the other post said and be sure the cable is not shorting out anywhere.  A few years after I did this I got lazy and put the car in the shop to get the cable replaced.  Told the guy to follow my cable only and not to get creative.  The next day on my way to work at 0530 I look in the mirror and see a bountiful cascade of sparks flying out of the vents.  New cable shorted out on the heat shield and cut 2 in. into the cable and caught on fire.  Guy got real pale when I told him he caught my Fiero no fire. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Pheagey||12-11-2001||09:04 PM||||   Cool, thanks. I geuss I'll get another one torrow and run it alone the firewall behind the heat shielding. How long do you suppose it will need to be?<BR>   I'm still open to other suggestions.<BR>   How expensive is heat shielding for wires? Is it any good?..<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||10
Z||000005||GTDude||12-11-2001||09:15 PM||||My guess is that the wire is not the proper gage for the distance it has to travel.  Used to see this alot on older Mopar cars.  They all had small gage wires.<P>Also, some heat shielding wouldn't hurt.  You could even make your own out of tin.  Or you can actually buy heat shielded cable with some sort of asbestos like material.<P>GTDude||||reg||1
Z||000006||Pheagey||12-11-2001||11:27 PM||||   Interesting, I think I'll go with that heat shielded cabling. I like the sound of that. Isn't the '+' wire a 8 or 6 gauge? Thanks...<P>------------------<BR>'88 coupe, 5 speed, custome paint job<BR>"I! have a plan!"<BR>Have you seen my website yet?<BR>Http://www.LithiumEffects/pheageysfeiro<BR>Killed: '97 civic CS auto;<BR>Killed by: '94 Civic Ex, '96 Camery LX<BR>Vandalised: 2 time (in 2 days).||||reg||1
